replacing std::cin with a custom fread-based scanner reduces I/O time dramatically, while the inner max-reduction loop remains auto-vectorizable due to independent iterations; adding an explicit omp simd reduction(max) clause ensures vectorization without violating dependencies.\" That combines both.\n\nBut we need to be careful: The current source uses std::cin. We can write a FastScanner class similar to typical competitive programming fast input using fread. Since the environment is ARM64 with GCC 12.2, fread should work. We must preserve correctness for all sizes/seeds/repeated calls. Input format: first line \"N S\", then N lines each containing N integers separated by whitespace (including newlines). FastScanner can read ints using getchar_unlocked? On Linux, getchar_unlocked is available but not standard; we can use fread buffer and manual parsing.\n\nWe need to output exactly one integer sum. The current code casts mxSum to int because result fits 32-bit signed. We'll keep that.\n\nLet's design FastScanner:\n\nclass FastScanner {\n    static const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    int idx, size;\n    FILE* f;\npublic:\n    FastScanner() : idx(0), size(0) { f = stdin; }\n    inline char getChar() {\n        if (idx >= size) {\n            size = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, f);\n            idx = 0;\n            if (size == 0) return 0;\n        }\n        return buf[idx++];\n    }\n    template<typename T>\n    bool readInt(T& out) {\n        char c;\n        T sign = 1;\n        T num = 0;\n        c = getChar();\n        if (!c) return false;\n        while (c != '-' && (c < '0' || c > '9')) {\n            c = getChar();\n            if (!c) return false;\n        }\n        if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\n        for (; c >= '0' && c <= '9'; c = getChar()) {\n            num = num * 10 + (c - '0');\n        }\n        out = num * sign;\n        return true;\n    }\n};\n\nBut note: after reading last digit, we consume one extra char that is whitespace; that's fine because next read will skip it. However if c==0 at end of file, loop stops.\n\nWe need to include <cstdio> and maybe <unistd.h>? fread from stdin works.\n\nThen in main:\nFastScanner fs;\nint n,s;\nif (!fs.readInt(n)) return 0;\nfs.readInt(s);\n...\nfor i,j: int x; fs.readInt(x);\n\nThis should be much faster than cin.\n\nNow, regarding SIMD: We can add `#pragma omp simd reduction(max:mxSum)` before inner loop. But note that mxSum is a reduction variable for outer parallel; inside the body it's private per thread. Adding omp simd on inner loop with reduction(max:mxSum) should be legal because each iteration of j updates mxSum independently and max is associative/commutative. However, OpenMP spec says if a variable appears in a reduction clause of an enclosing construct (outer parallel for), it cannot also appear in a reduction clause of an inner simd? Actually I think it's allowed: the outer reduction makes mxSum private to each thread; then within that thread, the inner simd can further reduce over j. But we need to ensure no race across threads because each thread has its own mxSum until end of parallel region. So yes.\n\nBut previous experiment e28e353d75544686 already did this and got same time as parent (0.0351). That suggests compiler already vectorized or the loop is not bottleneck. With FastScanner, compute becomes more significant relative to I/O? Actually with fast scanner, total time 0.0124; if we add SIMD maybe reduce compute further but likely negligible.\n\nMaybe we can also improve memory access by using `#pragma omp simd` and unrolling manually? But let's keep simple: FastScanner + explicit omp simd reduction(max) on inner loop. Also use restrict pointers to help vectorization.\n\nWe need to ensure the code compiles with GCC 12.2, OpenMP enabled (likely -fopenmp). The Makefile probably already has that; we don't modify it.\n\nLet's write the new solution.cpp:\n\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nclass FastScanner {\n    static const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    int idx, size;\npublic:\n    FastScanner() : idx(0), size(0) {}\n    inline char getChar() {\n        if (idx >= size) {\n            size = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n            idx = 0;\n            if (size == 0) return 0;\n        }\n        return buf[idx++];\n    }\n    template<typename T>\n    bool readInt(T& out) {\n        char c;\n        T sign = 1;\n        T num = 0;\n        c = getChar();\n        if (!c) return false;\n        while (c != '-' && (c < '0' || c > '9')) {\n            c = getChar();\n            if (!c) return false;\n        }\n        if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\n        for (; c >= '0' && c <= '9'; c = getChar()) {\n            num = num * 10 + (c - '0');\n        }\n        out = num * sign;\n        return true;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n, s;\n    if (!fs.readInt(n)) return 0;\n    fs.readInt(s);\n\n    const int W = n + 1;\n    std::vector<long long> pref((n + 1) * (n + 1), 0LL);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            int x;\n            fs.readInt(x);\n            row_sum += x;\n            pref[(i + 1) * W + (j + 1)] = pref[i * W + (j + 1)] + row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n    const int limit_i = n - s + 1;\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i < limit_i; ++i) {\n        const long long* __restrict__ row_pref = &pref[i * W];\n        const long long* __restrict__ bottom_pref = &pref[(i + s) * W];\n        #pragma omp simd reduction(max:mxSum)\n        for (int j = 0; j < limit_i; ++j) {\n            long long sum = bottom_pref[j + s] - row_pref[j + s] - bottom_pref[j] + row_pref[j];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::cout << static_cast<int>(mxSum) << '\\n';\n    return 0;\n}\n\nBut note: __restrict__ is GCC extension; should be fine. However, using restrict on pointers that point into same array but non-overlapping ranges may help compiler vectorize without worrying about aliasing.\n\nWe also need to include <cstdio> for fread and stdin.\n\nPotential issue: FastScanner::getChar returns char; if EOF returns 0 which is not a valid char? It's fine. readInt loops skip whitespace until digit or '-'. At end, after reading last int, it consumes one extra whitespace char (newline) but that's okay because next call will skip it.\n\nWe must ensure no undefined behavior: In for loop reading digits, when c is whitespace, the loop exits and we have consumed that whitespace. That means the next readInt starts at after whitespace; good.\n\nNow, does this preserve correctness? Yes.\n\nBut wait: The original code used std::cin with sync_with_stdio(false). Our FastScanner uses fread directly on stdin; mixing C++ iostream output (std::cout) is fine because we don't use cin anymore. We should not call ios::sync_with_stdio(false) maybe unnecessary but harmless. Actually if we use both printf and cout, need to be careful, but we only use cout for output. fread reads from stdin; no conflict.\n\nWe can remove iostream include? But we still use std::cout, so keep <iostream>. Also <cstdio> for fread.\n\nNow, is this transformation legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies? We added omp simd reduction(max) which is a vectorization directive. The restrict qualifiers help with aliasing analysis. So yes.\n\nWe should also consider that the outer parallel for already has reduction(max:mxSum); adding inner simd reduction might cause nested reductions but OpenMP allows it as long as mxSum is not shared across threads? Actually in OpenMP, a variable listed in reduction of an enclosing construct becomes private to each thread. Then inside the body, using #pragma omp simd reduction(max:mxSum) on a loop that updates mxSum shou